<DocPageMeta Title="Concurrency in Shift Framework"
             Section="Guides"
             Order="40"
             Status="DocPageStatus.Draft"
             TestedAgainst="2026.09.30.2"
             Description="What LastSaveDate does when two people edit the same record, what it does not do, and how to avoid the three traps behind this year's incidents: stale stamps after actions, silent overwrites, and long writes that get cancelled."
             Keywords="concurrency,conflict,409,lastsavedate,version,stamp,optimistic concurrency,cancellation,cancellationtoken,operation cancelled by user,batch,resumable,sync,lost update" />

<DocSection Title="In two minutes">
    <ul>
        <li><b>The check:</b> on <code>PUT</code>, the framework compares the record's <code>LastSaveDate</code> with the one
            the client sent, and answers <b>409 Conflict</b> if they differ. That is the whole mechanism.</li>
        <li><b>It only looks at the root.</b> Child records' stamps are never compared, so a stale child can overwrite a
            newer one without a 409.</li>
        <li><b>A save through the root's own form re-stamps the root</b> — even when nothing changed, and even when only
            a child changed. Every other open copy then gets a 409 for edits that never actually conflicted.</li>
        <li><b>The check is not in the database.</b> It runs in C# before the save, and the <code>UPDATE</code> checks no
            version, so two saves that arrive together can both pass and one edit is silently lost.</li>
        <li><b>An action that changes a record must answer with the new version</b>, or the form that ran it fails its
            next save with a 409.</li>
        <li><b>Long writes must commit in batches</b> that record their own progress, or a cancelled run loses all of it
            and every retry starts from zero.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note" Title="Where this comes from">
        Three production incidents this year, each rebuilt and proven against SQL Server with tests in the StockPlusPlus
        sample: a cancelled sync (A), silent overwrites (B), and a stale stamp after an action (C).
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What LastSaveDate does">
    <p>
        Every <code>ShiftEntity</code> has a <code>LastSaveDate</code>, and every view DTO carries it to the client. When the
        client saves with <code>PUT</code>, <code>ShiftEntityCrudHandler.PutAsync</code> loads the row and compares:
    </p>
    <DocCodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@(@"// ShiftEntity.Web/ShiftEntityCrudHandler.cs (abridged)
item = await repository.FindAsync(id);

if (item.LastSaveDate != dto.LastSaveDate)
    throw new ShiftEntityException(new Message(""Conflict"", ""...modified by another process...""),
                                   (int)HttpStatusCode.Conflict);

await repository.UpsertAsync(item, dto, ActionTypes.Update, userId);   // stamps a new LastSaveDate
await repository.SaveChangesAsync();")" />
    <p>
        This one check serves all three endpoint styles: secure controllers
        (<code>ShiftEntitySecureControllerAsync</code>), attribute endpoints
        (<code>[ShiftEntitySecureEndpoint&lt;…&gt;]</code>) and minimal APIs (<code>MapShiftEntitySecureCrud</code>).
        Your own endpoints and actions do <b>not</b> get it: they check nothing unless you do.
    </p>
    <p>Who moves the stamp:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>ShiftRepository.UpsertAsync</code> stamps the root it saves, on every update.</li>
        <li><code>ShiftRepository.SaveChangesAsync</code> stamps every added or modified auditable row in the unit of work
            — children and unrelated entities included.</li>
        <li>A save through a bare <code>DbContext</code> stamps <b>inserts only</b>; modified rows keep their old stamp.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What it does not do">
    <p>Four weaknesses, each reproduced by a diagnosis test against SQL Server (they pass by showing the flaw):</p>
    <ol>
        <li><b>A save that changes nothing still moves the stamp.</b> Tab A presses Save without editing; the stamp moves;
            tab B's real edit gets a 409 although no data changed.</li>
        <li><b>Saving a child through its parent moves the parent's stamp.</b> In the parent's form, children are saved
            with the parent's <code>PUT</code>, which always re-stamps the parent. Change one invoice line's price there,
            and another tab editing only the invoice header gets a 409.</li>
        <li><b>Stamps on child DTOs are never compared.</b> A child changed by another path — its own endpoint, or an
            action that changes only the child — gets a new stamp of its own, but the parent's stamp does not move. A tab
            holding the old line then saves the invoice with no 409 — and because lines are usually replaced on save, the
            newer value is simply gone.</li>
        <li><b>Two saves that both pass the check both commit.</b> Check and save are separate steps and no column is a
            database-checked version. Two requests that load the same version both pass, both save, both answer
            "saved" — and when they changed the same field, the first edit is lost.</li>
    </ol>
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Tip" Title="Different fields merge, the same field does not">
        EF Core only writes the columns whose value changed on the row each request loaded. Assigning a column the value
        it already has is not a change, so a mapper that copies every member of the DTO still writes only what the user
        edited. So in weakness 4, two racing saves that edit <i>different</i> fields both survive; an edit is lost when
        both change the <i>same</i> field. Don't rely on this: nothing tells the user their edit was overwritten.
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
        Weaknesses 1 and 2 make conflicts appear where there are none; weaknesses 3 and 4 hide conflicts that are real. The
        traps below are where users actually meet them.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Trap: an action that changes a child (Incident C)">
    <p>
        <b>What happened.</b> On an order form, a user ran an action that saved a child line. Their next edit to the order
        always failed with a 409, although nobody else had touched it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Why.</b> The action cancelled a line <i>and</i> recalculated the order's total. The order row itself changed, so
        the repository's save stamped it too: the order got a new <code>LastSaveDate</code>. (An action that changed only
        the line would not have moved the order's stamp — that is weakness 3 instead.) The action answered <code>204 No Content</code>, so the form kept the
        stamp it had loaded before. Its next <code>PUT</code> carried an out-of-date stamp: 409, every time.
    </p>
    <p><b>The fix.</b> An action that changes a record answers with that record — the root — as it now is, in the same
        shape as <code>GET</code>:</p>
    <DocSnippet Name="ConcurrencyActionReturnsCurrentRoot" Language="cs" />
    <p>
        The form must then take that version. <code>ShiftEntityForm</code> has no public way to accept a new value after it
        has loaded, so rebuild it by changing its <code>@@key</code>; the new instance loads the current record:
    </p>
    <DocCodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@(@"<ShiftEntityForm @key=""formVersion"" @bind-Value=""order"" @bind-Key=""Key"" Endpoint=""Order"" ...>
    ...
    <MudButton OnClick=""() => CancelLineAsync(line)"">Cancel line</MudButton>
</ShiftEntityForm>

@code {
    private int formVersion;

    private async Task CancelLineAsync(OrderLineDTO line)
    {
        using var response = await Http.PostAsync($""Order/{Key}/lines/{line.ID}/cancel"", null);
        if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
            formVersion++;   // rebuild: the form loads the order with its new LastSaveDate
    }
}")" />
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning" Title="Don't fix it by loosening the check">
        The form's old copy really is out of date: its line still says "not cancelled". Accepting its stamp would save that
        line back and silently undo the action (weakness 3). Keep the check strict; give the client the new version.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Trap: a long write that gets cancelled (Incident A)">
    <p>
        <b>What happened.</b> A sync wrote a large batch of rows to Azure SQL. It failed twice with
        <code>SqlException … Operation cancelled by user</code>, and each retry started again from zero.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>"Cancelled by user" is not a person.</b> It is SQL Server's answer when the <code>CancellationToken</code> passed to
        a command fires while the command is running: a request timeout, a client disconnect, a host shutdown. (Locally,
        EF often notices the token between commands and throws <code>TaskCanceledException</code> instead — same cause.)
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Why every retry started from zero.</b> All rows were written by one <code>SaveChangesAsync</code> — one
        transaction. A cancellation rolled back everything, the next run had the same amount of work and the same time
        budget, and failed the same way. One row the database refuses fails such a sync on every run, too.
    </p>
    <h3 class="mt-4">Recipe: a resumable, cancellable batch sync</h3>
    <p>Keep the sync's progress on the rows it processes, and write each row's result and its progress together:</p>
    <DocSnippet Name="ConcurrencyResumableInbox" Language="cs" />
    <p>Give each source row one result, enforced by the database:</p>
    <DocSnippet Name="ConcurrencySyncUniqueKey" Language="cs" />
    <p>
        Then process batches. Each batch's results and its rows' Done/Failed marks are one transaction. Only an error the
        row itself causes marks it Failed; anything else stops the run and leaves the batch Pending:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="ConcurrencyResumableSyncLoop" Language="cs" />
    <p>
        Let only one run work on a feed at a time. A caller that retries while the first run is still going, or a second
        server, would otherwise read the same Pending rows. A SQL Server application lock on the run's connection does
        this across processes, and the server drops it if the run dies:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="ConcurrencySyncRunLock" Language="cs" />
    <p>
        Always give the run a time budget shorter than any timeout around it, so it stops with an answer instead of an
        error. The server sets it: a caller that sends none gets the default, and none can ask for more than the maximum:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="ConcurrencySyncEndpoint" Language="cs" />
    <p>What this guarantees, proven against SQL Server (the first four with 10,000 rows in the StockPlusPlus sample's sync, the last two by running this recipe):</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Finished work is kept.</b> A cancel loses at most the batch in flight — never part of a batch.</li>
        <li><b>The caller learns how far it got.</b> A cancelled run answers <code>Cancelled</code> with what it did and
            <code>Remaining</code>; the counts use <code>CancellationToken.None</code> so they still work after a cancel.</li>
        <li><b>The next run resumes.</b> It starts at the first row still <code>Pending</code>: cancelled after 2,000 rows,
            the next run processed exactly the 8,000 left.</li>
        <li><b>Bad rows don't block the sync.</b> A row that fails validation, or that SQL Server refuses (found by retrying
            the batch row by row), is marked <code>Failed</code> with the reason and never retried; the rest finish.</li>
        <li><b>Good rows are never failed by accident.</b> A deadlock, a timeout or a lost connection is not the row's
            fault: the run stops, the batch rolls back, and the rows stay <code>Pending</code> for the next run.</li>
        <li><b>Runs don't overlap.</b> A second run on the same feed answers <code>AlreadyRunning</code> at once, and the
            unique key stops a duplicate result even if something else writes too.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Tip" Title="No inbox table?">
        When the source is not yours to mark, keep a checkpoint row instead (the last committed key per feed) and update it
        in the same transaction as each batch. The rule is the same: progress and results commit together.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Trap: two editors at once (Incident B)">
    <ul>
        <li><b>Expect false 409s after no-op saves and child saves</b> (weaknesses 1–2). They are safe — nothing is lost —
            but users read them as "someone else edited this". Avoid saving when nothing changed.</li>
        <li><b>Change a child in one place.</b> If a child can be edited both inside its parent's form and by its own
            action or endpoint, the parent's form can overwrite it silently (weakness 3). Route child changes through one
            path, and make that path return the parent.</li>
        <li><b>Don't trust the check under load.</b> Two simultaneous saves of the same record can both succeed (weakness
            4). For records where a lost edit is costly, see the direction below.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Checklist">
    <ul>
        <li>Every custom action that changes a record answers with that record's root as it now is.</li>
        <li>Every form that runs such an action takes the answer (or reloads) before the next save.</li>
        <li>Never relax the <code>LastSaveDate</code> comparison to make a 409 go away.</li>
        <li>Long writes commit in batches that carry their own progress, and report <code>Remaining</code> when cancelled.</li>
        <li>A row that can never be processed is marked, not retried forever — and only errors the row causes mark it.</li>
        <li>Only one run per feed at a time, and the database refuses a second result for the same source row.</li>
        <li>Pass the caller's <code>CancellationToken</code>, and always add a time budget shorter than the timeouts around
            you; the server sets it, not the caller.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Where the framework could go">
    <p>These are directions, not current behaviour:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Stamp only real changes.</b> Skip re-stamping the root when nothing on it changed, and decide deliberately
            whether a child change moves the parent's version (weaknesses 1–2).</li>
        <li><b>Check the version in the database.</b> Marking <code>LastSaveDate</code> (or a <code>rowversion</code>) as an EF
            concurrency token makes a racing stale save fail with <code>DbUpdateConcurrencyException</code> instead of
            committing (weakness 4). Nothing translates that exception into a 409 today, so it needs framework support
            before apps can rely on it.</li>
        <li><b>Version the aggregate, not just the root</b>, or compare child stamps (weakness 3).</li>
        <li><b>Let forms take a new version.</b> A public way to hand <code>ShiftEntityForm</code> the record an action
            returned, and an action helper that returns the root by default, would make Incident C's fix the default.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>
